extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Loading indicators"

block content

	//- One demo pair (Panel + PanelSet) per section, so the three ways can be compared like for
	//- like. Everything that differs between them is passed in: a class carrying CSS variables,
	//- or the attributes that take the library's own indicator out.
	mixin loadingDemo(n, opts)
		- opts = opts || {}
		.grid-2.gap-v-1
			div
				h3 Panel
				.demo(class=opts.demoClass)
					button(aria-controls=`li${n}-panel` aria-expanded="false") Load pricing
					div(id=`li${n}-panel` data-panel data-panel-custom-indicator=opts.custom data-panel-loading-height=opts.height)
						.panel-wrapper
							//- filled in by example-loading.js

			div
				h3 PanelSet
				.demo(class=opts.demoClass)
					.tabs(role="tablist" aria-label=`Loading indicators demo ${n}`)
						button(role="tab" aria-controls=`li${n}-tab-1` aria-selected="true") Features
						button(role="tab" aria-controls=`li${n}-tab-2` aria-selected="false") Pricing
					div(id=`li${n}-tabs` class="tab-content" data-panelset data-panel-custom-indicator=opts.custom data-loading-height=opts.height)
						.panel-wrapper
							div(id=`li${n}-tab-1` role="tabpanel" class="active" data-loaded="true")
								+example-1(4)
							div(id=`li${n}-tab-2` role="tabpanel" hidden)
								//- filled in by example-loading.js

	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Loading indicators
				p.lead The one piece of design the library ships, and how to replace it.


	+section("The exception to unstyled")

		p Panel and PanelSet ship #[a(href=url('/how-it-works.html#unstyled')) no styling], with one exception: the loading indicator. While async/remote/slow content loads, you get a spinner, and it has a size, a thickness, a speed and colors.

		p The spinner is there because the library needs it. Panel and PanelSet need to show #[em at least something] to indicate to the user that there is some waiting to be expected. That "at least something" is the spinner. You do not have to use it, but you do have to show something. NOT showing anything if there is async/remote/slow content loading in the background is worse.

		p You will probably want one of three things, and they are the three sections below:

		ol
			li #[strong The default spinner], in the panel area. You get it out of the box.
			li #[strong Your own spinner], with your own size, thickness, speed and colors. You can do that with CSS variables.
			li #[strong Your own indicator]: a spinner in the trigger, a progress bar, skeleton content, a label change. You can do that with the state the library exposes.

		.callout
			+warning()
			p The library supplies the #[strong state], not the look. #[code .is-loading] on the panel, #[code aria-busy] and #[code .is-trigger-loading] on the trigger. The spinner is only the default way of showing that state. Replace it and nothing else changes.


	+section("1. The default spinner")

		p Nothing to set up. Load content asynchronously and the spinner appears in the panel area, in both components:

		+loadingDemo(1)

		p It only appears if the load takes longer than #[code loadingDelay] (320ms by default), so a fast load opens straight to its content without a flash of spinner. While it shows, the panel holds #[code loadingHeight] (150px by default) so there is somewhere for it to live, and the old content dims behind it.

		+codeblock('JavaScript').
			new Panel('#my-panel', {
				loadingDelay: 320,   // wait this long before showing anything
				loadingHeight: 150   // the room it holds while it waits
			});


	+section("2. Your own spinner")

		p Same spinner, your values. Every part of it is a CSS variable, so nothing needs to be rebuilt. The pair below is the demo above with five variables changed:

		+loadingDemo(2, { demoClass: 'li-tuned', height: 100 })

		+codeblock('CSS').
			.my-panel {
				--ps-spinner-size: 24px;
				--ps-spinner-thickness: 8px;
				--ps-spinner-track-color: pink;
				--ps-spinner-color: limegreen;
				--ps-spinner-speed: 1.4s;
			}

		p Set them wherever they inherit from: on #[code :root] for every panel on the page, or on one element to change just that one.

		h3 CSS variables

		.card.api
			dl
				dt.code --ps-spinner-size
				dd How big the circle is. Default #[code 40px].

				dt.code --ps-spinner-thickness
				dd How thick the ring is. Default #[code 4px].

				dt.code --ps-spinner-track-color
				dd The ring that stays put. Default #[code rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)].

				dt.code --ps-spinner-color
				dd The arc that travels, which is what reads as movement. Default #[code rgba(0, 100, 255, 0.8)].

				dt.code --ps-spinner-speed
				dd One full turn. Default #[code 0.8s].

				dt.code --ps-spinner-fade-in-speed
				dd How long it takes to fade up once it appears, so it does not arrive as a flash. it is unset by default, which means it follows whatever the spinner appears alongside: the content dimming in a PanelSet (#[code --ps-loading-dim-duration]), or the panel opening in a Panel (#[code --ps-open-speed]). Give it a value to pick one number for both.

				dt.code --ps-spinner-fade-out-speed
				dd How long it takes to fade away once the content is there. Default #[code 0.2s]. Separate from the one above, so the spinner can come up gently and leave quickly.


				dt.code --ps-spinner-top / --ps-spinner-bottom
				dd Defaults: #[code 50%] and #[code auto].

				dt.code --ps-spinner-left / --ps-spinner-right
				dd Defaults: #[code 50%] and #[code auto].

				dt.code --ps-spinner-transform
				dd Default #[code translate(-50%, -50%)], which pulls the circle back by half its own size so that #[code 50%/50%] is its middle rather than its top-left corner. To place it against an edge instead, set this to #[code translate(0, 0)]. It has to stay a transform function: #[code none] is not valid here, because the spin is composed on top of it.

				dt.code --ps-loading-panel-opacity
				dd How far the old content fades back while the new content loads. Default #[code 0.1]. Set it to #[code 1] to leave the content alone.

				dt.code --ps-loading-dim-duration
				dd How long that fade back takes (PanelSet). Follows #[code --ps-panel-out-speed] unless you set it, and the spinner fades in over the same time, so the two read as one cross-fade.


	+section("3. Your own indicator")

		p If a spinner in the panel area is not what you want at all, take it out and show the wait yourself. In the pair below the spinner sits in the trigger instead, the button of the Panel and the tab of the PanelSet, and the content area holds no room for one:

		+loadingDemo(3, { demoClass: 'uses-trigger-spinner', custom: true, height: '0' })

		p Two things make that work:
		ul
			li #[code customIndicator] tells the library you are showing the state yourself, so it does not show its spinner.
			li #[code loadingHeight: 0] is the other half: with the indicator on the trigger, the content area does not need to hold space for one, so it stays collapsed until the real content arrives. That is why the demos above open straight to their content. 

		+codeblock('JavaScript').
			new Panel('#my-panel', {
				customIndicator: true,
				loadingHeight: 0
			});
			new PanelSet('#my-panelset', {
				customIndicator: true,
				loadingHeight: 0
			});

		p Or use it as one-offs, as data-attributes:

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<div data-panel data-panel-custom-indicator data-panel-loading-height="0"> … </div>
			<div data-panelset data-panel-custom-indicator data-loading-height="0"> … </div>

		p After that, you can use the following states and classes for styling:

		.card.api
			dl
				dt.code .is-loading
				dd On the panel or the panelset, while its content loads.

				dt.code aria-busy="true"
				dd On every trigger pointing at that panel. This is the real accessibility signal for a control whose content is being fetched, so it is worth keying off rather than the class.

				dt.code .is-trigger-loading
				dd On those same triggers, for when you want a class instead.



		+codeblock('CSS').
			/* show it on the trigger: a button, or a tab */
			[aria-busy="true"]::after {
				content: "";
				display: inline-block;
				width: 14px;
				height: 14px;
				margin-left: 0.5em;
				border: 2px solid rgb(0 0 0 / 0.15);
				border-top-color: currentColor;
				border-radius: 50%;
				animation: my-spin 0.6s linear infinite;
			}

			@keyframes my-spin {
				to { rotate: 360deg; }
			}

		p Both components mark that state while content loads, and clear it the moment the load settles, whether it finishes, is cancelled, or fails.

		p It does not have to be a spinner. The state is just a state: fade the trigger, put a progress bar above the panel, show skeleton content, swap the label. The library does not care what you show.

	//- What every demo on this page loads.
	template#li-content
		+example-2(4)


append scripts
	if isProd
		script(src=`${basePath}lib/panelset.js` vite-ignore)
		script.
			Panel.init('.docs-content [data-panel]');
			PanelSet.init();
		script(src=`${basePath}assets/scripts/example-loading.js` vite-ignore)
	else
		script(type="module").
			import { Panel, PanelSet } from 'panelset';
			Panel.init('.docs-content [data-panel]');
			PanelSet.init();
		script(type="module" src="/src/assets/scripts/example-loading.js")
